            <ArticleTitle>Seeing the difference-Painless progressive vision loss in a vasculopath.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0039-6257(17)30229-1</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.survophthal.2017.09.001</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 78-year-old Caucasian man with significant vascular disease reported sudden onset of worsened vision during a routine wound-care appointment for nonhealing necrotic leg ulcers. He described painless blurring of vision with grey scotomas in his right eye, his only well-seeing eye, after trauma to the left eye as a child. He presented with retinal ischemia, a cotton wool spot, and optic nerve swelling. Temporal artery biopsy showed myxoid degeneration and extensive calcium deposition, which also was present on imaging throughout his carotid and vertebral arterial system-the clinical picture of calciphylaxis.</AbstractText>
